  "details": "<p>This archive is located in the Nab<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-0c1c18ae-7fff-5320-6d88-0d39b5a48d5d\">\u00fb temple.</span></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"514\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan73_Nabu_temple_Dur-Sharrukin1-2_p157.png\" width=\"507\" /><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-0c1c18ae-7fff-5320-6d88-0d39b5a48d5d\"><br /></span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Two tiers of niches (similar to Dur-\u0160arrukin 1) were found in Room H 15 (on the northwestern side of Courtyard II) of the Nab\u00fb temple at Dur-\u0160arrukin, the short-lived capital of the Assyrian king Sargon II (721\u2013705 BC). Although no inscribed objects were found inside the niches, the existence of a temple library is suggested by a similar construction in Room H 5 (on the northwestern side of Forecourt I). The empty niches might be the result of the move of the capital to Nineveh under Sennacherib (704\u2013681 BC) or of the use of perishable writing materials.",
  "reprPoint": [
